# omahouse — what a package cannot do from a file list.
#
# Five things: the two directories of docs/design.md §4, the service of §9, the
# one line of PAM that makes `logout` mean anything at all (§2), and the meter of
# §5.2 -- which is signed here, on the machine, because the key that decides its
# extension id is made here and did not exist when the package was built. The PAM
# line and the meter are the two that write files this package does not own, and
# between them they are the reason this script is longer than it looks like it
# should be.
#
# Everything here is idempotent. It runs again on every upgrade, and a machine
# that already has the line has to come out of it unchanged -- including one
# where somebody moved the line by hand, which is theirs to have done.
#
# And removal is not an afterthought here, it is half the job. omahouse puts
# things on a machine that a file list cannot take back off it, and every one of
# them is a restriction: a restriction that outlives the program that made it is
# worse than one that never existed, because there is no longer anything on the
# machine that knows how to lift it. `_artifacts` below is the list of all of
# them, and it is the only place any of those paths is written down.

# -- the prefix ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Empty on a real machine, and that is the whole of what it does there.
#
# It is here so that this scriptlet can be *run* -- `post_install` and then
# `post_remove` -- against a directory of its own, which is what
# `check_the_removal_covers_what_the_install_makes` in `tests/test_cli.py` does
# on every commit. Without it, the only way to find out whether removal is
# complete is to install the package on a machine and take it off again, which
# is a VM run, which is nightly and manual -- and the failure this whole half of
# the file exists to prevent is silent and on the wrong side. It was found once
# exactly that way, in a VM, and only because somebody thought to look.
#
# It is the same variable `/usr/lib/omahouse/meter-pack` reads, so a rooted run
# of this script passes the root down to the packer through the environment
# without either of them having to know about the other. The same seam
# `$OMAHOUSE_CONFIG_DIR` and `$OMAHOUSE_CHROMIUM_POLICY_DIR` are for the program
# itself, and it carries the same discipline: a run pointed at a tree of its own
# writes where it was told to write, and it does not touch this machine's
# services.
_root="${OMAHOUSE_PACK_ROOT:-}"

# -- the one list -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **Everything the installation puts on a machine outside its own file list, and
# what removal does about it.** Declared once, here, and read by two things: the
# `post_remove` below, which does what the first column says; and a test in
# `tests/test_cli.py`, which runs this whole script against a temporary root and
# refuses a path that appeared on disk and is not named here.
#
# That pairing is the point, and it is a correction of something that had gone
# wrong. The `.crx`, the update manifest, the force-install policy, the native
# messaging manifest and the signing key are made by a scriptlet rather than
# shipped, so `pacman -Ql` does not list them and `pacman -Qkk` does not check
# them -- the package is no longer describable by its own file list. What stood
# in for that was a column of hand-written `rm` lines, and the VM proved on the
# very first run that the column was already incomplete: the meter's native
# messaging host survived `pacman -R`, on a machine with nothing left to explain
# or undo it. A hand-written list is not a thing to write more carefully. It is a
# thing to stop having.
#
# So: adding an artefact and forgetting to remove it now breaks the gate, and
# not somebody's machine.
#
# Three verbs, and the third is not tidiness:
#
#   take    removed, whatever else is kept. Every restriction is one of these.
#   prune   a directory of ours: removed if it is empty, left alone if somebody
#           put something in it. Order matters -- deepest first.
#   borrow  a directory that belongs to another program and that this install
#           creates if it is not there. Never removed, and the test requires
#           that nothing of omahouse's is left inside it.
#   keep    left on purpose, and named in the removal message with the words in
#           the third column. A report is evidence and outlives the rules it was
#           collected under.
#
# A `take` line may hold a glob, and is expanded against the prefix.
_artifacts() {
	cat <<-'LIST'
	take    /etc/omahouse/blocked
	take    /etc/chromium/policies/managed/omahouse.json
	take    /etc/chromium/policies/managed/omahouse-meter.json
	take    /etc/chromium/native-messaging-hosts/com.omahouse.meter.json
	take    /usr/share/omahouse/chromium/omahouse-meter.crx
	take    /usr/share/omahouse/chromium/updates.xml
	take    /etc/omahouse/meter
	take    /run/user/*/omahouse/focus
	prune   /run/user/*/omahouse
	prune   /usr/share/omahouse/chromium
	prune   /usr/share/omahouse
	borrow  /etc/chromium/policies/managed
	borrow  /etc/chromium/native-messaging-hosts
	keep    /etc/omahouse                 the directory the two below live in
	keep    /etc/omahouse/profiles.json   who was under rules
	keep    /var/lib/omahouse             the days already counted
	LIST
}

# Why each `take` line is a `take`, said once here rather than beside a `rm`:
#
# **`blocked`** is the one thing in this file that must not be left behind. A
# machine with a name still in it and no omahouse installed is a machine where
# nothing can ever take that name back out -- the daemon that would have done it
# at the turn of the day is gone. Removing the package has to be a way out, not
# a way to make the lock permanent.
#
# **The browser policy of §11** is the same argument with a different file. A
# site blocked by omahouse is blocked by a file in another program's directory,
# and Chromium goes on reading that file whether or not omahouse is installed.
# Leaving it is a machine where a site does not open, the browser says "managed
# by your organisation", and no program on the disk can explain it or undo it.
#
# **The meter's four files** are the same argument again. A force-install policy
# left behind after the package is gone is a browser that goes on installing an
# extension whose native host is not on the disk, reporting into nothing, with
# nothing left that knows how to take it off.
#
# **The key** -- `/etc/omahouse/meter` -- is the one a file list could never have
# removed, because a build never knew it would exist (§5.2, "The signing key").
# It goes for a reason stronger than tidiness: the whole of what a key per
# machine buys is that nothing anywhere can sign an extension this machine's
# policy would accept, and a key left on the disk of a machine with no omahouse
# on it is exactly that thing, waiting for somebody to write a policy by hand.
# The price is stated and not hidden -- a reinstall makes a new key, so a new id,
# so a new extension -- and it is affordable only because this extension keeps no
# state at all.
#
# **The focus files** go with the session anyway, since /run/user is a tmpfs
# systemd empties at logout, but a machine that has not been rebooted since the
# uninstall would still have them, and they name sites somebody visited.
#
# And the two directories under /etc/chromium are `borrow` and not `prune`: they
# existed before this package on any machine with a browser on it, Omarchy's own
# `browser-policy.sh` writes `policies.json` beside our file, and they are not
# ours to take. Everything omahouse ever put in them is on a `take` line above.

_take_what_the_install_made() {
    local verb path rest
    while read -r verb path rest; do
        case "$verb" in
        take)
            # Unquoted on purpose: `/run/user/*/omahouse/focus` is a glob, and a
            # glob that matches nothing passes through as itself, which `rm -rf`
            # treats as a path that is not there. Every path here is a literal in
            # the list above and none of them has a space in it.
            rm -rf -- $_root$path
            ;;
        prune)
            rmdir $_root$path 2>/dev/null || true
            ;;
        esac
    done < <(_artifacts)
}

# -- the PAM line -------------------------------------------------------------

_pam_file="$_root/etc/pam.d/system-login"
_pam_marker='pam_listfile.so.*file=/etc/omahouse/blocked'

# The line poc/findings.md round 3 measured, and the only shape of it that is
# ever written.
#
#   account required pam_listfile.so item=user sense=deny \
#           file=/etc/omahouse/blocked onerr=succeed
#
# `onerr=succeed` is NOT OPTIONAL, and it is not a preference. It is what makes a
# missing or unreadable /etc/omahouse/blocked let everybody in. Without it, a
# file that is not there yet -- which is every machine between this scriptlet and
# the first time somebody runs out of time -- would refuse every login on the
# system, at the console, with no way in to fix it. The same reasoning runs
# through the whole of omahouse: the failure that leaves the rules soft is
# recoverable, and the one that leaves them hard is not. docs/design.md §2 and §4 both
# say it, and this is the place where getting it wrong costs somebody their
# machine rather than an afternoon.
#
# The path inside the line is never prefixed, and a rooted run writes the same
# bytes a real one would: what is under test is the edit, and a PAM line
# pointing into somebody's $TMPDIR would be a line no machine will ever have.
_pam_line='account    required   pam_listfile.so item=user sense=deny file=/etc/omahouse/blocked onerr=succeed'

_install_pam_line() {
    if [ ! -f "$_pam_file" ]; then
        cat >&2 <<-MESSAGE
		>>> omahouse: $_pam_file is not there, so the login block was not installed.
		>>>           Budgets with onExhausted "logout" will end the session and not
		>>>           stop the next login. Add this line to your PAM login stack:
		>>>
		>>>           $_pam_line
		MESSAGE
        return
    fi

    # Already there, however it got there. A second copy would refuse the same
    # people twice and would be one more line for the next upgrade to remove.
    if grep -Eq "^[^#]*$_pam_marker" "$_pam_file"; then
        return
    fi

    # Before every other `account` line, so that a refusal happens before
    # anything else in the stack has a chance to succeed.
    local temporary
    temporary="$(mktemp "${_pam_file}.omahouse.XXXXXX")" || return
    awk -v line="$_pam_line" '
        !done && /^[[:space:]]*account[[:space:]]/ { print line; done = 1 }
        { print }
        END { if (!done) print line }
    ' "$_pam_file" > "$temporary" || { rm -f "$temporary"; return; }

    # A truncated PAM file is a machine nobody can log into, so the new one is
    # checked for the line it was supposed to gain and for the lines it was
    # supposed to keep before it replaces anything.
    if [ "$(wc -l < "$temporary")" -le "$(wc -l < "$_pam_file")" ] \
        || ! grep -Eq "^[^#]*$_pam_marker" "$temporary"; then
        rm -f "$temporary"
        echo ">>> omahouse: could not edit $_pam_file; it was left alone." >&2
        return
    fi

    chmod --reference="$_pam_file" "$temporary" 2>/dev/null || chmod 0644 "$temporary"
    chown --reference="$_pam_file" "$temporary" 2>/dev/null || true
    mv -f "$temporary" "$_pam_file"
}

_remove_pam_line() {
    [ -f "$_pam_file" ] || return
    grep -Eq "^[^#]*$_pam_marker" "$_pam_file" || return

    local temporary
    temporary="$(mktemp "${_pam_file}.omahouse.XXXXXX")" || return
    grep -Ev "^[^#]*$_pam_marker" "$_pam_file" > "$temporary" || { rm -f "$temporary"; return; }
    chmod --reference="$_pam_file" "$temporary" 2>/dev/null || chmod 0644 "$temporary"
    chown --reference="$_pam_file" "$temporary" 2>/dev/null || true
    mv -f "$temporary" "$_pam_file"
}

_directories() {
    # 0644 files in 0755 directories -- docs/design.md §4. Root writes both and
    # everybody reads them, which is what lets the fiscalised user run
    # `omahouse status` and see what is left of their own day.
    #
    # Both are `keep` lines in `_artifacts`: they hold the profiles and the days
    # already counted, and a report is evidence that outlives the rules it was
    # collected under.
    install -d -m 0755 "$_root/etc/omahouse"
    install -d -m 0755 "$_root/var/lib/omahouse"
}

# The meter, signed here rather than in a build -- docs/design.md §5.2, "The
# signing key". `/usr/lib/omahouse/meter-pack` makes this machine's own key if
# there is not one yet, signs the extension into a `.crx` with it, and writes the
# force-install policy and the native messaging manifest that name the id that
# key produces. It is idempotent: on an upgrade it finds the key already there,
# so the id does not move and the browser sees a version going up rather than a
# second extension appearing beside the first.
#
# Everything it writes is on a `take` line above, and the test is what makes that
# sentence true rather than hopeful: it runs this function for real and then
# looks at what appeared.
#
# A failure here is loud and does not stop the transaction. Everything else
# omahouse does -- the apps, the budgets, the session -- works without a browser
# extension; what is lost is time per site, and half an install is worse than an
# install that says which half is missing.
_pack_meter() {
    local said
    if [ ! -x "$_root/usr/lib/omahouse/meter-pack" ]; then
        return
    fi
    # The packer reads `$OMAHOUSE_PACK_ROOT` out of the environment, which is
    # where this script read `$_root` from, so a rooted run reaches it without
    # either of them naming the other.
    if said="$("$_root/usr/lib/omahouse/meter-pack" 2>&1)"; then
        echo ">>> omahouse: the browser meter is signed for this machine and forced" >&2
        echo ">>>           into Chromium. Its extension id here is ${said##*$'\n'}" >&2
        return
    fi
    # No unquoted heredoc here, and no backticks anywhere in the text. The
    # comment above `post_install` is the story of a message that ran its own
    # words; this one carries a captured error string, which is somebody else's
    # text and the worst possible thing to hand to the shell.
    {
        echo ">>> omahouse: could not sign the browser meter, so there is no time per"
        echo ">>>           site on this machine. Everything else works. It said:"
        echo ">>>"
        printf '>>>           %s\n' "$said"
        echo ">>>"
        echo ">>>           openssl and zip are what it needs. Run"
        echo ">>>           /usr/lib/omahouse/meter-pack again once they are there."
    } >&2
}

# The service, and only on a run that is managing this machine.
#
# A run pointed at a tree of its own is not; enabling a unit out of a test would
# be the same mistake `mayTouchTheBrowserPolicy` refuses in the CLI, with a
# longer fuse, because a leftover enabled unit on a developer's laptop starts at
# the next boot and nothing says why.
_service() {
    [ -z "$_root" ] || return 0
    systemctl "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

post_install() {
    _directories
    _install_pam_line
    _pack_meter

    # Enabled, and not started. A daemon that begins counting inside a pacman
    # transaction is a daemon nobody chose to run yet, and there are no profiles
    # on a machine that has just installed this -- `watch` would find an empty
    # list and say so. It starts at the next boot, or when somebody asks.
    #
    # Enabling at all is the deliberate part: docs/design.md §9. A stopped daemon is a
    # rule switched off, and this package exists to be a rule.
    _service enable omahouse.service

    # Quoted delimiter. The text below names verbs in backticks, and an
    # unquoted heredoc would run them: measured on a real Omarchy install, where
    # `pacman -U` executed `omahouse profile enforce` out of its own welcome
    # message and printed a parse error for the `<user>` it read as a redirect.
    # Nothing here needs expansion, so nothing here gets it.
    cat <<-'MESSAGE'
	>>> omahouse: /etc/omahouse and /var/lib/omahouse are ready, and
	>>>           omahouse.service is enabled for the next boot.
	>>>
	>>>           Nobody is under rules yet. Start with:
	>>>             omahouse profile add <user> --name "Name"
	>>>             omahouse status <user>
	>>>
	>>>           A new profile only counts and reports. Read a day of
	>>>           `omahouse report <user>` before `omahouse profile enforce
	>>>           <user> --on` puts the teeth in.
	MESSAGE
}

post_upgrade() {
    _directories
    # Again on every upgrade, because a PAM file replaced by a .pacnew merge is a
    # machine where the line quietly went away and nothing else would notice.
    _install_pam_line
    # And again for the same reason on the browser's side. The extension's source
    # is a file this package owns, so an upgrade has just replaced it: without
    # this line the machine would go on force-installing an archive signed from
    # last version's source, and `chrome://extensions` would show a version the
    # package no longer ships. The key is not touched, so the id does not move.
    _pack_meter
}

pre_remove() {
    _service disable --now omahouse.service
}

post_remove() {
    _remove_pam_line

    # Everything on the list, in the order the list is written. There is no
    # column of `rm` lines here any more, and that is the change: a path that is
    # not on the list is not removed, and a path that is not on the list and
    # appears on a machine turns the gate red.
    _take_what_the_install_made

    # And any meter host that is **still running**, which is not a file and is
    # the one thing here a file list could never have suggested.
    #
    # A native messaging host lives as long as the pipe the browser gave it, and
    # a browser that was open through the removal still holds that pipe. Nothing
    # else on the machine would end it: the process is the child's, it holds a
    # `/usr/bin/omahouse` that no longer has a name, and it goes on appending to
    # the focus file the list above has just deleted -- so the file comes back
    # within five seconds, naming the sites she is visiting, on a machine with no
    # omahouse on it and nothing left to explain either. That is precisely the
    # failure the whole of this function refuses, and it is not a file, which is
    # why no list of paths catches it.
    #
    # By exact command line and not by name: `omahouse` alone would take a
    # `status` somebody is reading in another terminal. The native messaging
    # manifest is gone by now, so nothing can spawn another one after this, and
    # `/usr/bin/omahouse` is not on the disk any more either.
    #
    # Only on a run that is managing this machine, for the reason `_service`
    # gives: a rooted run has no business killing processes that are nothing to
    # do with the tree it was pointed at.
    if [ -z "$_root" ]; then
        pkill -f '^/usr/bin/omahouse meter' 2>/dev/null || true
        pkill -f '^/bin/sh /usr/lib/omahouse/meter-host' 2>/dev/null || true
        # And the same process named by the one argument only a browser ever
        # passes it, whatever path it was reached through. Nothing but a native
        # messaging host is ever called with an extension origin on its command
        # line.
        pkill -f 'omahouse meter chrome-extension://' 2>/dev/null || true
    fi

    cat <<-'MESSAGE'
	>>> omahouse: everything omahouse put on this machine is off it.
	>>>
	>>>           The login block is gone and everybody can log in again, the
	>>>           browser policy in /etc/chromium/policies/managed/omahouse.json
	>>>           is gone and every site opens again, the meter extension is no
	>>>           longer force-installed, its native host is gone, the key this
	>>>           machine signed it with is gone, and the service is stopped and
	>>>           disabled. No rule omahouse wrote is still in force.
	>>>
	>>>           Chromium drops a force-installed extension the next time it
	>>>           starts without a policy naming it. If a window was open through
	>>>           all of this, close it once.
	>>>
	>>>           Kept on purpose, and here is where they are:
	MESSAGE
    # Out of the same list `post_remove` just worked from, so the message cannot
    # come to name a path the removal treats differently.
    _artifacts | awk '$1 == "keep" {
        path = $2
        $1 = ""; $2 = ""
        sub(/^ +/, "")
        printf ">>>             %-29s %s\n", path, $0
    }'
    cat <<-'MESSAGE'
	>>>           A report is evidence, and it outlives the rules it was
	>>>           collected under. Nothing there does anything to the machine
	>>>           now; `rm -rf` all three if you want the account back to nothing.
	MESSAGE
}
